### Why don't Kafka transactions cover a database write plus a Kafka publish?

A Kafka transaction gives atomicity over records written to Kafka topics and offsets committed for consumed messages. The transaction coordinator and commit markers are entirely inside Kafka. A relational database has its own completely separate transaction manager. There is no shared coordinator, and a distributed two-phase commit across both is impractical.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class DualWriteProblem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Naive approach 1: DB first, then Kafka");
        System.out.println("  DB commits -> crash -> Kafka never gets the event");
        System.out.println("  Result: LOST EVENT");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Naive approach 2: Kafka first, then DB");
        System.out.println("  Kafka commits -> crash -> DB never gets the row");
        System.out.println("  Result: PHANTOM EVENT (consumers acted on nothing)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Both orderings are unsafe. This is the dual-write problem.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Naive approach 1: DB first, then Kafka
  DB commits -> crash -> Kafka never gets the event
  Result: LOST EVENT

Naive approach 2: Kafka first, then DB
  Kafka commits -> crash -> DB never gets the row
  Result: PHANTOM EVENT (consumers acted on nothing)

Both orderings are unsafe. This is the dual-write problem.
```

### How does the transactional outbox pattern work?

You give your database an outbox table. In one local transaction, write the business change and an outbox row. A relay reads unpublished rows and publishes to Kafka.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.UUID;

public class TransferServiceWithOutbox {

    // @Transactional -- ONE local DB transaction
    public void transfer(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Business rows
        // accountRepo.debit(fromAccount, amount);
        // accountRepo.credit(toAccount, amount);

        // Outbox row -- same transaction
        String eventId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        // outboxRepo.save(new OutboxEvent(eventId, "TransferCompleted",
        //     buildPayload(fromAccount, toAccount, amount)));

        System.out.println("DB transaction committed:");
        System.out.println("  Debit: " + fromAccount + " -" + amount);
        System.out.println("  Credit: " + toAccount + " +" + amount);
        System.out.println("  Outbox: " + eventId + " TransferCompleted");
        System.out.println("  No Kafka call here -- relay publishes from outbox");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransferServiceWithOutbox service = new TransferServiceWithOutbox();
        service.transfer("SAV2001", "SAV3001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
DB transaction committed:
  Debit: SAV2001 -50000
  Credit: SAV3001 +50000
  Outbox: a1b2c3d4-... TransferCompleted
  No Kafka call here -- relay publishes from outbox
```

### What does the outbox table look like?

```sql
CREATE TABLE outbox_events (
    id          UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    aggregate_id VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,  -- e.g., account id for ordering
    event_type  VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    payload     TEXT NOT NULL,
    created_at  TIMESTAMP DEFAULT NOW(),
    published   BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE
);

-- Unique constraint prevents duplicate outbox entries
-- aggregate_id used as Kafka key for per-account ordering
```

